# Findings reviewed and dismissed, one exact fingerprint each.
#
# A fingerprint is `<commit>:<path>:<rule>:<line>`, so an entry here dismisses
# ONE finding in ONE historical commit. It is the narrowest mechanism gitleaks
# offers, and the narrowness is the point: the alternatives all suppress things
# nobody has looked at. Disabling `generic-api-key` would hide every future
# key; lowering entropy would hide them globally; allowlisting the path or the
# directory would hide whatever lands there next; allowlisting the commit would
# hide everything else in the same commit.
#
# `.gitleaks.toml` holds the allowlists that are about a *class* of file -- the
# profile templates, the test fixtures, and one real historical leak whose
# credentials were destroyed. This file holds individual false positives.
#
# Why a fingerprint and not a `#gitleaks:allow` comment on the line: the
# Security workflow scans history with `fetch-depth: 0`. A comment lives in the
# working tree, and the blob as it existed in the offending commit does not
# contain it -- so the comment would silence the finding on the current commit
# and the historical one would fail the job forever.
#
# An entry is only valid for the commit it names. If the file is edited the
# line moves, a new fingerprint appears, and it is reviewed again rather than
# inherited. That is intended.

# generic-api-key, entropy 3.56, in the notification recipient resolver:
#
#     _platform_addresses(
#         organization_id=organization_id, token=token, permission=...
#     )
#
# `token` is a parameter being passed through to a helper -- the function's own
# argument, named the same as the keyword it fills. There is no literal here
# and nothing to rotate. The value it eventually carries is a platform token
# minted at request time and never stored in this repository.
#
# Reviewed 2026-09-20, after it failed the Security workflow on every push
# since 8d7ed3b. False positive.
